= NEWSNIPER Ethical Public License, Version 1 — Extension EP (NEPLv1-EP)

#strong[NEWSNIPER Ethical Public License v1 — Extension EP] · draft, first revision (not frozen; wording may change)

#strong[EP stands for Explicit Permission.] That one requirement is what this Extension is.

#strong[This is a translation; the Korean text is authoritative] (clause E8.1).

#strong[This document is an "Extension" to NEPLv1] (NEPLv1 clause 10.4). It does not replace that licence and #strong[has effect only where it is applied together with it.]

This Extension does one thing: #strong[it stops another person's words from being rewritten without their explicit permission.]

#line(length: 100%, stroke: 0.4pt + gray)

== E0. About this Extension

E0.1 #strong[Basis.] This Extension is published by the Licence Steward under clause 10.4 of NEPLv1. As that clause provides, this Extension #strong[does not replace NEPLv1], #strong[has effect only where it is applied together with NEPLv1], and everything not stated here remains exactly as NEPLv1 provides.

E0.2 #strong[It is optional.] This Extension binds only a work #strong[whose Application Notice states "Extensions: NEPLv1-EP".] Where the Application Notice carries no such entry, this Extension does not exist (NEPLv1 clause 10.4).

E0.3 #strong[This Extension does only two things] — exactly what clause 10.4 of NEPLv1 permits an Extension to do.

(a) #strong[It removes the making of adaptations from the grant in clause 2.2] — but only as to the "Speaker's Wording" defined in E1.1 below (E2).

(b) #strong[It adds one condition to the attribution required when that wording is excerpted or rearranged] (E3.4).

E0.4 #strong[This Extension deletes nothing from NEPLv1 and weakens nothing in it.] In particular the #strong[Prohibited Uses in clause 5] are not altered in the slightest by this Extension, as the last sentence of NEPLv1 clause 10.2 already requires.

E0.5 #strong[This Extension grants no new rights whatsoever.] It only takes away. The scope of use of a work carrying this Extension is therefore #strong[always narrower than, or the same as,] that of a work carrying NEPLv1 alone.

E0.6 #strong[Non-binding note.] This Extension brings NEPLv1 closer to a state of "readable but not rewritable". What NEPLv1 says about itself in clause 0.1 therefore applies here too, and more strongly — #strong[it is not "open source" as defined by the OSI, and not "free software" as defined by the FSF.] This clause aids interpretation only and creates no rights or obligations.

#line(length: 100%, stroke: 0.4pt + gray)

== E1. Definitions

E1.1 #strong["The Speaker's Wording"] (below, "that wording") means #strong[those parts which the Application Notice designates as subject to this Extension.] The typical case is a record of what one person said in their own words — a lecture, dictation, interview, consultation, written answer — but it is not confined to that.

#strong[This Extension does not bind any part the Application Notice has not designated.] Within the same repository, therefore, the Licensor's own prose may be adapted on NEPLv1's ordinary terms.

E1.2 #strong["The Speaker"] means the person who spoke or wrote that wording, and any successor designated by them in writing under E5.2. #strong[This may be a different person from the Licensor under NEPLv1 clause 1.7] — the person who prepares and distributes a record need not be the person who spoke.

E1.3 #strong["Altering the wording"] means changing the #strong[words, sentences, order or sense] of that wording. As in NEPLv1 clause 12.1, #strong[differences of layout, line breaks, markup, typeface or file format are not changes to the wording.]

E1.4 #strong["Correction by juxtaposition"] means recording an error, a supplement or a dissent #strong[in a separate document placed alongside] that wording, without altering it (E5.1).

#line(length: 100%, stroke: 0.4pt + gray)

== E2. Narrowing of the grant — adaptations

E2.1 #strong[As to that wording, the making of adaptations and the use of such adaptations is removed from the grant in NEPLv1 clause 2.2.] That is, #strong[rewriting that wording is not permitted at all.]

E2.2 #strong[It may nonetheless be done to the extent the Speaker has expressly permitted in writing] (E4).

E2.3 #strong[Legal character.] The narrowing in E2.1 concerns #strong[the scope of the grant made by clause 2 of NEPLv1.] Distributing that wording in altered form without permission is therefore #strong[a use that was never licensed], and constitutes #strong[copyright infringement.]

#emph[(This mirrors NEPLv1 clauses 5.5 and 9.3. Only where the governing law does not recognise it as a limitation of scope, or characterises it as a contractual obligation, does the narrowing subsist as a contractual obligation owed by You to the Speaker and the Licensor.)]

E2.4 #strong[The cure in NEPLv1 clause 8.2 therefore does not apply to this narrowing.] The use lies outside the scope of the grant, so no rights under the licence ever existed and there is nothing to reinstate. #emph[(This is the same position as NEPLv1 clause 8.3 takes for clause 5.)]

E2.5 #strong[This narrowing prevails over the accommodation in NEPLv1 clause 4.] A Rightsholder of the Upstream Work is likewise given no right to alter that wording.

#strong[The reason is simple] — the additional permission in NEPLv1 clause 4.1 is #strong[granted by the Licensor], and the Licensor #strong[was never in a position to authorise the adaptation of the Speaker's Wording.] This is the same logic by which clause 6.2 states that the Licensor grants rights only in their own creative contribution.

E2.6 #strong[This Extension does not bind the Software Part.] The grant in NEPLv1 clause 2.1 stands. However, a file designated by the Application Notice as that wording is subject to this Extension regardless of whether it is a Software Part under NEPLv1 clause 1.3.1.

#line(length: 100%, stroke: 0.4pt + gray)

== E3. Outside the reach of this Extension

E3.1 #strong[Quotation has nothing to do with this Extension.] Quotation, criticism, research and reporting within the limits the law of the place of use places on economic rights (e.g. Korean Copyright Act art. 28) #strong[require no permission in the first place], so this Extension does not narrow them. NEPLv1 clause 6.3 says the same.

E3.2 #strong[Translation is not an alteration of the wording.] Rendering it faithfully into another language is not "altering" within E1.3 and #strong[requires no permission from the Speaker.] The translation permission in NEPLv1 clause 2.2 survives intact.

A translation shall state that it is a translation and what the original was.

E3.3 #strong[Adaptation into another idiom, however, is an alteration.] Reworking the content to fit another context, audience or medium falls within E2.1 and #strong[requires permission.] The line between a faithful rendering and a fitted one is the line this Extension draws.

E3.4 #strong[Excerpt and rearrangement.] Carrying across only part of that wording is not barred by this Extension, #strong[so long as the wording of the part carried across is not altered.] In that case, however, #strong[the following shall also be shown] — that it is an excerpt, and #strong[where it came from.] #emph[(This is the condition referred to in E0.3(b); it is one line added to the attribution required by NEPLv1 clause 3.1.)]

#emph[(An excerpt cut so far that the sense changes is not an excerpt but an adaptation. That judgement is made by reference to "sense" in E1.3.)]

E3.5 #strong[Format conversion is not adaptation.] In the same sense as NEPLv1 clause 12.1 provides for the licence text, moving that wording into another document format, encoding or medium is not a change to the wording.

#line(length: 100%, stroke: 0.4pt + gray)

== E4. How permission is sought

E4.1 #strong[Permission is obtained in writing] (including electronically). The person seeking it shall state #strong[what they propose to change and how.]

E4.2 #strong[Silence by the Speaker is neither consent nor refusal.] #emph[(This is the same idiom as NEPLv1 clause 1.9(d).)] While no reply has come, that wording may not be altered — but correction by juxtaposition under E5.1 is always available.

E4.3 #strong[A permission extends only as far as it says.] One permission is not to be read as permitting later, different changes.

E4.4 #strong[A permission creates no conditions beyond this Extension.] In granting it the Speaker may not impose restrictions that NEPLv1 does not provide (NEPLv1 clause 2.5, last sentence).

#line(length: 100%, stroke: 0.4pt + gray)

== E5. Where the Speaker cannot be reached

E5.1 #strong[Correction by juxtaposition is always available.] Typographical errors, errors of fact, matters overtaken by time, and dissents are recorded #strong[in a separate document placed alongside, instead of by altering that wording.] That document shall state #strong[who wrote it and when], and #strong[shall not be presented as the Speaker's words.]

#emph[(This is what keeps this Extension from becoming a dead end. The wording is locked and #strong[the way to set it right stays open.])]

E5.2 #strong[Successors.] The Speaker #strong[may designate a successor in writing.] Where they die or become untraceable without such a designation, that wording is dealt with by correction by juxtaposition under E5.1 alone.

E5.3 #strong[And ceasing distribution is always available.] Nothing in this Extension prevents the Licensor from ceasing to distribute that wording. Under NEPLv1 clause 2, however, #strong[the rights of those who lawfully obtained it beforehand are unaffected.]

#line(length: 100%, stroke: 0.4pt + gray)

== E6. The Speaker's other rights

E6.1 #strong[This Extension does not displace moral rights.] The rights of attribution and integrity, and equivalent rights, that the Speaker holds under the law of the place of use stand independently of this Extension.

E6.2 #strong[This Extension creates no right of revocation.] The grant in NEPLv1 clause 2 remains #strong[irrevocable], and this Extension gives nobody a right to take back permissions already granted. #strong[What this Extension does is withhold from the outset, not reclaim what was given.]

E6.3 #strong[This Extension gives the Speaker no exclusive right to interpret this licence.] #emph[(As in NEPLv1 clause 1.9(d).)]

#line(length: 100%, stroke: 0.4pt + gray)

== E7. Application Notice — how to use this Extension

Fill in the #strong["Extensions" field] of the Application Notice under NEPLv1 clause 12, and add the following.

#block(width: 100%, fill: luma(245), inset: 8pt, radius: 2pt, raw("Extensions:        NEPLv1-EP\nExtension text:    <path>\n\nSpeaker:           <name, or the designation they choose>\n                   NB: if they do not wish to be named, record that instead.\nNotices to Speaker:\n                   <address or e-mail>   (where permission under E4 is sought)\nSpeaker's successor:\n                   <name · address>      (E5.2 — leave blank if none designated)\n\nSubject of this Extension (the Speaker's Wording):\n                   <paths or scope>\n                   NB: anything not listed here is not bound (E1.1)."))

E7.1 #strong[If no subject is listed, this Extension binds nothing.] It is not a device for locking a whole repository.

E7.2 #strong[No entry in the Application Notice can overturn E0.4.] Clause 5 of NEPLv1 is relaxed neither by this Extension nor by the Application Notice.

#line(length: 100%, stroke: 0.4pt + gray)

== E8. Versions and translations

E8.1 #strong[The Korean text of this Extension is authoritative.] The English, Japanese and German texts are convenience translations; in case of divergence, the Korean text prevails.

E8.2 #strong[Fixed versions.] New versions of this Extension are published #strong[only by the Licence Steward (NEPLv1 clause 1.12).] #strong[Only the version stated in the Application Notice applies; no option such as "or any later version" is provided.]

E8.3 #strong[NEPLv1 clause 10.3 applies to this Extension document as it stands] — it may be copied and distributed verbatim and anyone may apply it to their own work, but #strong[a modified version shall not use the abbreviation "NEPL".] Translating it into another language is permitted and such a translation may bear this name.

E8.4 #strong[Recommendation (non-binding).] In machine-readable metadata, the use of `LicenseRef-NEPL-1.0-EP` is recommended. This is a recommendation; not following it is not a breach (NEPLv1 clauses 12.1 and 12.2).

#line(length: 100%, stroke: 0.4pt + gray)

== E9. Spirit — non-binding postscript

#strong[When you carry across what someone said, you do not carry across the right to change it.] If something in it is wrong, do not correct it in place; write beside it. Then their words remain as they were, and so does the error.

#strong[Better that both remain.]

This clause creates no rights or obligations.

